;;; A relation parameter its activator binds to `eq` heads a conclusion of one
;;; argument, and root stored `(eq a)` — regression
;;; `eq-arity-conclusion` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A rule `r` whose parameter `?R` heads its conclusion, `(?R ?x)`, and its
;;; activator `(r eq)`: for every `p`, `r` concludes `(eq ?x)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `eq` is the relation of equality, between exactly two values, whose facts
;;; are computed and never stored (Q-M1e2.26), and the
;;; grammar refuses `(eq ?x)` as a conclusion. Written through the parameter
;;; and an activator the program states, it is refused at load (Q-M1f.46), as
;;; the same parameter heading a premise is (`eq-arity-premise.ein`), and
;;; every command that loads the program exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program loaded and root stored `(eq a :rule r)`, a fact of `eq` at one
;;; argument (`ein saturate FILE --dump`), no conclusion checked; `eq` holds
;;; no candidate, so a state's `(model …)` did not name it, and R was two
;;; states, ∅ and `{(p z)}`, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE`
;;; at `ee3da657`, release and debug alike).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The loader refuses the program: every command that loads it exits 1 with
;;; one line, naming `r`, `?R`, the form and `(r eq)` (`ein test`: 2), in
;;; either build. The regression's `now` is that refusal; the section above is
;;; the record of the answer it replaced.
;;;

(relation p Node :why "{?3} is a p")
(p a)
(hit z)
(rule r (?R)
  :match (p ?x)
  :assert (?R ?x)
  :why "{?x} is a p, so it stands in {?R}")
(r eq)
(query :goal (p ?x))
